### A · The waves in general

1. A **wave** of feminism is a period of intense activity with shared demands; the metaphor was popularised by **Martha Weinman Lear** in ==1968==.
2. The **first wave** (c. ==1848==–==1920==) fought for the **vote**, property, education and legal personhood.
3. The **second wave** (==1960s==–==1980s==) fought over **work, family, sexuality and the body**.
4. The **third wave** (==1990s==–==2000s==) stressed **diversity, identity and intersectionality**.
5. The **fourth wave** (from about ==2012==) is defined by **digital activism** against harassment and violence.
6. Critics say the wave metaphor follows **white, Western, middle-class** history and hides activism between waves.

### B · The first wave

7. **Coverture** meant a married woman's legal identity was **covered by her husband's**.
8. **Stanton and Mott** were excluded from the **World Anti-Slavery Convention** in London (==1840==) and resolved to hold Seneca Falls (MCQ 2024).
9. The **Seneca Falls Convention** was held on ==19–20 July 1848== in New York.
10. The Seneca Falls Convention was led by **Elizabeth Cady Stanton** (MCQ 2025).
11. The **Declaration of Sentiments** declared: "all **men and women** are created equal".
12. **Frederick Douglass** signed the Declaration of Sentiments.
13. **Susan B. Anthony** and Stanton founded the **National Woman Suffrage Association** in ==1869==.
14. **Wyoming** Territory gave women the vote in ==1869==.
15. The **19th Amendment** gave American women the vote in ==1920==.
16. **New Zealand** was the first self-governing country to give women the national vote (==1893==), after **Kate Sheppard**'s campaign.
17. **Finland** (==1906==) was the first European country to give women the vote and the right to stand.
18. The **NUWSS** (==1897==), led by **Millicent Fawcett**, were the peaceful **suffragists**.
19. The **WSPU** (==1903==), led by **Emmeline Pankhurst**, were the militant **suffragettes** — "**Deeds, not words**".
20. The "**Cat and Mouse Act**" (==1913==) released hunger-striking suffragettes and re-arrested them.
21. **Emily Wilding Davison** died after running onto the course at the Epsom **Derby** in ==1913==.
22. British women over **30** with property won the vote in ==1918==; equal votes at **21** came in ==1928==.
23. **Caroline Norton**'s campaign led to the **Custody of Infants Act** (==1839==).
24. Britain's **Married Women's Property Acts** were passed in ==1870== and ==1882==.
25. **Elizabeth Blackwell** was the first woman to earn a medical degree in the USA (==1849==).
26. **Huda Shaarawi** founded the **Egyptian Feminist Union** in ==1923==.

### C · The second wave

27. "**The personal is political**" is most associated with **second-wave** feminism (MCQ 2026).
28. **Betty Friedan**'s *The Feminine Mystique* (==1963==) described "the problem that has no name".
29. The **National Organization for Women (NOW)** was founded in ==1966==.
30. The US **Equal Pay Act** was passed in ==1963==; **Title VII** of the Civil Rights Act in ==1964==.
31. The contraceptive **pill** was approved in the USA in ==1960==.
32. The 1973 US Supreme Court case that legalised abortion was ***Roe v. Wade*** (MCQ 2025).
33. *Roe v. Wade* was overturned by ***Dobbs v. Jackson*** in ==2022==.
34. The **Miss America protest** (==1968==) used a "freedom trash can"; no bras were actually burned.
35. The **Ford Dagenham** machinists' strike (==1968==) led to Britain's **Equal Pay Act 1970**.
36. Britain's first **National Women's Liberation Conference** met at **Ruskin College**, Oxford, in ==1970==.
37. The first modern **women's shelter** opened in **Chiswick, London**, in ==1971==.
38. **Gloria Steinem** co-founded ***Ms.*** magazine (==1972==).
39. The US **Equal Rights Amendment** passed Congress in ==1972== but was never ratified; **Phyllis Schlafly** led the opposition.
40. The "**Manifesto of the 343**" (France, ==1971==) was signed by women who admitted illegal abortions.
41. On ==24 October 1975== about **90%** of Icelandic women went on a "day off".
42. **Susan Faludi**'s *Backlash* (==1991==) described the counter-attack on feminism in the 1980s.

### D · The third wave

43. The **Anita Hill** hearings (==1991==) sparked the third wave.
44. The term **Third Wave** feminism was coined by **Rebecca Walker** (MCQ 2025) in *Ms.* (==1992==).
45. Walker wrote: "I am not a post-feminism feminist. I am the **Third Wave**."
46. **Riot Grrrl** (from ==1991==) used punk music and **zines** to spread feminism.
47. **Naomi Wolf** wrote *The Beauty Myth* (==1990==).
48. **Eve Ensler**'s *The Vagina Monologues* (==1996==) led to the global **V-Day** campaign.
49. The US **Violence Against Women Act** was passed in ==1994==.
50. **Postfeminism** claims equality has been won; **Angela McRobbie** criticised it.
51. "**Choice feminism**" — calling any individual choice feminist — is a common criticism of the third wave.
52. **Women Living Under Muslim Laws** (==1984==) is a transnational network linking activists, including Pakistanis.

### E · The fourth wave

53. **Kira Cochrane** (*All the Rebel Women*, ==2013==) argued a fourth wave had begun.
54. The phrase "**Me Too**" was first used by **Tarana Burke** in ==2006==; **#MeToo** went global in **October** ==2017==.
55. **SlutWalk** began in **Toronto** in ==2011==.
56. The **Everyday Sexism Project** was founded by **Laura Bates** in ==2012==.
57. **#NiUnaMenos** ("Not one less") began in **Argentina** in ==2015==.
58. India's **Criminal Law (Amendment) Act 2013** followed protests over the Delhi gang rape of ==2012==.
59. Ireland removed its constitutional ban on abortion by referendum on ==25 May 2018==.
60. Iran's "**Woman, Life, Freedom**" protests began in September ==2022==.
61. Pakistan's mobile-internet gender gap fell from ==38%== to ==25%== (GSMA, 2024–25).

### F · The United Nations

62. The **UN Charter** (==1945==) affirms "the **equal rights of men and women**".
63. The **Commission on the Status of Women** was set up in ==1946==.
64. **International Human Rights Day** is observed on **10 December** (MCQ 2018) — the date of the UDHR (==1948==).
65. ==1975== was **International Women's Year**; ==1976–1985== was the **UN Decade for Women**.
66. The first UN conference on women was held in **Mexico City** (==1975==) (MCQ 2016).
67. The second conference was held in **Copenhagen** (==1980==), where CEDAW was opened for signature.
68. The third conference, in **Nairobi** (==1985==), adopted the **Forward-Looking Strategies**.
69. The **Fourth World Conference on Women** was held in **Beijing** (MCQ 2019) in ==1995==.
70. The Beijing Declaration and **Platform for Action** was adopted in ==1995== (MCQs 2024, 2026).
71. The Beijing Platform has **12 critical areas of concern** and was adopted by **189** governments.
72. The **Beijing** conference identified women's vital role in **environmental management** (MCQ 2023) — critical area K.
73. Beijing endorsed **gender mainstreaming**.
74. **CEDAW** was adopted on ==18 December 1979== and entered into force on ==3 September 1981==.
75. CEDAW is often described as **an international bill of rights for women** (MCQ 2026).
76. Pakistan **acceded** to CEDAW in ==1996== (MCQs 2016, 2018, 2019), on **12 March**.
77. Pakistan made a **general declaration** (subject to the Constitution) and a **reservation to Article 29(1)**.
78. CEDAW **Article 4** permits **temporary special measures** such as quotas.
79. CEDAW **Article 14** concerns **rural women**; **Article 16** concerns marriage and family.
80. CEDAW's **Optional Protocol** (==1999==) allows individual complaints; Pakistan has not joined it.
81. The **Vienna** Conference (==1993==) declared "**women's rights are human rights**"; DEVAW followed in December ==1993==.
82. **UN Security Council Resolution 1325** (==2000==) concerns **Women, Peace and Security**.
83. **UN Women** was created in ==2010==; its first head was **Michelle Bachelet**.
84. **SDG 5** (==2015==) aims to "achieve **gender equality** and empower all women and girls".
85. **Beijing+30** was reviewed at the CSW in March ==2025==.
86. **DAWN** (==1984==) is a network of Southern feminists.

### G · Roots in South Asia

87. The reformer who advocated women's education and opposed **sati** was **Raja Ram Mohan Roy** (MCQ 2026); *sati* was banned in ==1829==.
88. **Ishwar Chandra Vidyasagar** campaigned for the **Hindu Widows' Remarriage Act** (==1856==).
89. **Sayyid Mumtaz Ali** wrote ***Huquq-un-Niswan*** (==1898==) and ran *Tahzib-e-Niswan* with **Muhammadi Begum**.
90. The **Child Marriage Restraint Act** ("Sarda Act") was passed in ==1929==.
91. The **Dissolution of Muslim Marriages Act** was passed in ==1939==.
92. The Muslim League formed a **Central Women's Sub-Committee** at **Patna** in ==1938==.
93. **Begum Jahanara Shahnawaz** was the only Muslim woman delegate at the **Round Table Conferences**.
94. **Jahanara Shahnawaz** and **Shaista Ikramullah** were the first women in Pakistan's **Constituent Assembly** (==1947==).
95. **Fatima Sughra** hoisted the Muslim League flag on the **Punjab Civil Secretariat** in ==1947==.
96. **Fatima Jinnah** is called ***Madar-e-Millat*** and contested the presidential election of ==1965==.

### H · The movement in Pakistan

97. The **All Pakistan Women's Association (APWA)** was formed in ==1949== (MCQ 2018).
98. APWA was founded by — and its first president was — **Begum Ra'ana Liaquat Ali Khan** (MCQs 2016, 2019).
99. The **West Punjab Muslim Personal Law (Shariat) Application Act 1948** gave women inheritance in **agricultural land**.
100. PM **Bogra**'s second marriage (==1955==) led to the **Rashid Commission** on marriage and family laws.
101. The **Muslim Family Laws Ordinance** was promulgated in ==1961== under **Ayub Khan**.
102. The **Hudood Ordinances** were enacted in ==1979== (MCQ 2016) under **Zia-ul-Haq** (MCQ 2019).
103. A major focus of Pakistan's women's movement in the **1980s** was the **Hudood Ordinances** (MCQ 2025).
104. The **Women's Action Forum** was founded in **Karachi** in ==1981== after the **Fehmida–Allah Bux** case.
105. On **12 February 1983** police baton-charged WAF and women lawyers in **Lahore**; the day became **National Women's Day** in ==2012==.
106. The **Safia Bibi** case (==1983==) exposed how the Zina Ordinance punished rape victims.
107. The **Qanun-e-Shahadat** (Law of Evidence) was enacted in ==1984==.
108. **Khawar Mumtaz and Farida Shaheed** wrote *Women of Pakistan: Two Steps Forward, One Step Back?* (==1987==).
109. **Benazir Bhutto** became the first woman prime minister of a Muslim country in ==1988==.
110. The **First Women Bank** opened in ==1989==; the first **women's police station** in ==1994==.
111. The **NCSW** was set up in ==2000==; **60** reserved NA seats came in ==2002==.
112. The **Protection of Women Act 2006** moved rape from the Zina Ordinance to the **Penal Code**.
113. The **Women's Parliamentary Caucus** was formed in ==2008==; **Fahmida Mirza** became the first woman Speaker.
114. The killing of **Qandeel Baloch** (==2016==) spurred the **honour-killing** amendment of October ==2016==.
115. The first **Aurat March** was held on ==8 March 2018==.
116. The slogan "***Mera Jism Meri Marzi***" ("My body, my choice") provoked controversy in ==2019==–==20==.
117. The **Sindhiani Tehreek** (from ==1982==) was a mass movement of rural Sindhi women.
118. **Al-Huda** was founded in ==1994== by **Farhat Hashmi**.
119. **Asma Jahangir** co-founded the **HRCP** (==1987==) and AGHS Legal Aid.
120. **Sharmeen Obaid-Chinoy**'s *A Girl in the River* (Oscar ==2016==) added pressure for the honour-killing law.

### I · NGO-isation and evaluation

121. **NGO-isation** means a movement turning into **professional, donor-funded NGOs** with projects and upward accountability.
122. **Sonia Alvarez** (==1999==) and **Islah Jad** (==2004==) wrote on NGO-isation.
123. **WAD** (Women and Development) says women were always part of development but on **unequal, exploitative** terms.
124. **Shirkat Gah** was founded in ==1975==; **Aurat Foundation** in ==1986==.
125. The **Sindh Home-Based Workers Act** (==2018==) was the first such law in Pakistan.
126. The movement's greatest weakness is the **gap between laws and enforcement**; its greatest strength, its **local and constitutional legitimacy**.
